{
  "version": "2.0.0",
  "cells": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "## IDL 9.3 Demo - Weather Data Visualization",
        "",
        "> This requires IDL 9.3 in order to run",
        "",
        "This notebook demonstrates:",
        "",
        "1. Installing and using Python's weather data libraries (OpenMeteo)",
        "2. Fetching real temperature data for multiple Colorado cities",
        "3. Visualizing the data with IDL's 3D surface graphics",
        "4. Saving the plot to disk in our new 3D formats",
        "",
        "**Note:** We'll use the `openmeteo-requests` package - a free, reliable weather API with no authentication required!",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "### Step 1: Install and Import Python Libraries",
        "",
        "First, we'll install the openmeteo-requests package and import the required libraries:",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; Install openmeteo package for weather data",
        "PyUtils.pipInstall, 'openmeteo-requests'",
        "print, 'Libraries installed successfully!'"
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "### Step 2: Fetch Weather Data for Colorado Cities",
        "",
        "We'll retrieve temperature data for multiple Colorado cities to create a spatial-temporal surface:",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; Import Python libraries using dot notation",
        "openmeteo = Python.import('openmeteo_requests')",
        "",
        "; Setup OpenMeteo API client with cache",
        "om = openmeteo.client()"
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "Specify the cities and date range for fetching weather data."
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; Define Colorado Front Range cities (latitude, longitude)",
        "city_names = ['Denver', 'Boulder', 'Colorado Springs', 'Fort Collins', 'Pueblo', $",
        "  'Greeley', 'Longmont', 'Loveland', 'Castle Rock', 'Aurora', $",
        "  'Lakewood', 'Thornton', 'Westminster', 'Broomfield', 'Centennial']",
        "lats = [39.7392, 40.0150, 38.8339, 40.5853, 38.2544, $",
        "  40.4233, 40.1672, 40.3978, 39.3722, 39.7294, $",
        "  39.7047, 39.8680, 39.8366, 39.9208, 39.5786]",
        "lons = [-104.9903, -105.2705, -104.8214, -105.0844, -104.6091, $",
        "  - 104.7091, -105.1019, -105.0749, -104.8561, -104.8319, $",
        "  - 105.0814, -104.9719, -105.0372, -105.0866, -104.8769]",
        "",
        ";+ start date",
        "start_str = '2026-06-01'",
        "",
        ";+ end date",
        "end_str = '2026-07-01'"
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "Use the Python libraries to fetch the data",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; Alert user",
        "print, 'Fetching temperature data'",
        "",
        "; Collect temperature data for all cities",
        "temp_list = list()",
        "for i = 0, n_elements(lats) - 1 do begin",
        "  ; alert user",
        "  print, ` For ${city_names[i]} (${i+1} of ${n_elements(lats)})`",
        "",
        "  ; Create parameters - pass IDL values to Python dict",
        "  params = orderedhash( $",
        "    'latitude', lats[i], $",
        "    'longitude', lons[i], $",
        "    'start_date', start_str, $",
        "    'end_date', end_str, $",
        "    'daily', 'temperature_2m_mean')",
        "",
        "  ; Fetch data from OpenMeteo",
        "  response = om.weather_api('https://archive-api.open-meteo.com/v1/archive', params = params)",
        "  daily = response[0].daily()",
        "",
        "  ; extract data",
        "  temp_data = (daily.variables(0)).valuesAsNumpy()",
        "  temp_list.add, temp_data",
        "endfor",
        "print, 'Done!'",
        "",
        "; Convert list to 2D array",
        "temp = temp_list.toArray()"
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "Next, Create a grid from the temperature data so that we can make a nice surface",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; Grid city temperatures onto a regular spatial grid for 2D visualization",
        "; Compute mean temperature across all days -> [n_cities]",
        "temp_mean = mean(temp, dimension = 2)",
        "",
        ";+ lon/lat spacing",
        "delta = 0.01",
        "",
        ";+ lon/lat buffer",
        "buff = 3 * delta",
        "",
        "; Define output grid bounds covering the Colorado region",
        "lon_range = [min(lons) - buff, max(lons) + buff]",
        "lat_range = [min(lats) - buff, max(lats) + buff]",
        "",
        "; grid size derived from extent and spacing",
        "nx = ceil((lon_range[1] - lon_range[0]) / delta) + 1",
        "ny = ceil((lat_range[1] - lat_range[0]) / delta) + 1",
        "",
        "; get 1D X/Y position of cells",
        "lon_grid = lon_range[0] + findgen(nx) * (lon_range[1] - lon_range[0]) / (nx - 1)",
        "lat_grid = lat_range[0] + findgen(ny) * (lat_range[1] - lat_range[0]) / (ny - 1)",
        "",
        "; Build 2D output grids",
        "lon_2d = rebin(lon_grid, nx, ny)",
        "lat_2d = rebin(reform(lat_grid, 1, ny), nx, ny)",
        "",
        "; Interpolate sparse city points onto the regular grid using inverse distance weighting",
        "temp_grid = griddata(lons, lats, temp_mean, $",
        "  xout = lon_2d, yout = lat_2d, $",
        "  /inverse_distance, power = 2)",
        "",
        "; re-grid for 2D",
        "temp_grid = reform(temp_grid, [nx, ny])",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "### Step 4: Create 3D Surface Plot",
        "",
        "Next we will create our visualizations of temperature variations across Colorado cities over time",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "First, to add some depth to our 3D surface, let's also overlay a color table so we have the z-axis and a color to make our plot look a little nicer."
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; load a color table to create a texture for the surface",
        "loadct, 16, rgb_table = ct",
        "red = ct[*, 0]",
        "green = ct[*, 1]",
        "blue = ct[*, 2]",
        "",
        ";+ byte-scale the temperature data so we can apply a color table",
        "temp_byte = bytscl(temp_grid, $",
        "  min = min(temp_grid, max = maxTemp), max = maxTemp)",
        "",
        ";+ generate RGB data",
        "rgb = [[[red[temp_byte]]], [[green[temp_byte]]], [[blue[temp_byte]]]]"
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "Next, we create the surface and embed in the notebook:",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; Create an interactive 3D surface plot",
        "s = surface(temp_grid, lon_2d, lat_2d, $",
        "  title = 'Temperature in Colorado', $",
        "  xtitle = 'Lon', $",
        "  ytitle = 'Lat', $",
        "  ztitle = 'Temperature (C)', $",
        "  texture_image = rgb)"
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": [
        "### Step 5: Save the Plot to Disk",
        "",
        "Finally, we'll save the weather visualization to disk using our new 3D export format",
        ""
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "content": [
        "; Define output path",
        "outputFile = routine_dir() + 'colorado_weather_surface.gltf'",
        "",
        "; Save with high resolution",
        "s.save, outputFile",
        "",
        "print, 'Weather surface plot saved to: '",
        "print, outputFile"
      ],
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": []
    }
  ]
}